{#- _partials/_data_quality.py.j2 — inline Glue Data Quality: evaluation, the
    row-level Passed/Failed split, the quarantine write, and the DQ report row
    (P28 R11.1; extracted by p64 R6.1/R6.2).

    EXTRACTED, NOT REWRITTEN. Every line below was moved verbatim out of
    glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2 (regions :174-271 and :275-345 as they stood).
    p64 2b.1 was a pure extraction whose acceptance test was that
    glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2 rendered BYTE-IDENTICALLY before and after, over
    the full source_format x checkpoint_enabled x write_mode x dqdl_rules x
    quarantine_bucket x partition_columns x column_rename_block matrix. 2b.2
    ported it to the other four templates; 2b.4 added the `data_quality_enabled`
    gate below.

    THE GATE, AND WHY IT IS AN `and` ON THE EXISTING GUARD (p64 R6.6 / O4).
    Both stages' bodies are behind
    `{% if data_quality_enabled is defined and data_quality_enabled and dqdl_rules … %}`.
    2b.2 made this block reachable from every target type, which — ungated —
    would give an EXISTING pipeline of any other target type row-level
    splitting, a quarantine write and a DQ verdict it never had, and would move
    `shared/quality_verdict.py`'s reported mode from advisory to `enforced` for
    a pipeline whose behaviour nobody changed. R6.6 forbids that.

    `data_quality_enabled` is resolved ONCE, by
    `code_assembly.resolve_data_quality_enabled`, and defaults to the posture
    each template had before p64 — on for glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2, off for the
    other four — so an existing pipeline of ANY target type renders exactly what
    it rendered before. Read that function for why a flat `False` default was
    rejected: it would have stripped the block from every existing S3 Tables
    pipeline, which is the same silent reclassification pointing the other way.

    `is defined and`, NOT THE DEFAULT-VALUE FILTER, AND NOT A BARE `{% if %}`.
    Three options, and the middle one is forbidden outright: the default-value
    filter is do-NOT 6 — under two jinja2 copies in one process it hands a foreign
    `missing` sentinel straight through, which is how all 18 tests in
    `test_code_generation_e2e.py` broke once (see `_load_counts.py.j2`'s header).
    `test_dq_template_vars_contract.py` scans this file for that filter's name and
    the count must stay zero, which is why this paragraph does not spell it —
    a fence asserted by substring counts its own documentation as a violation
    (`LGR-b46a5bb-4`, and the note at the foot of this header).

    A BARE `{% if data_quality_enabled %}` was the first choice and was reverted:
    under StrictUndefined it makes an omitted variable a loud render error, which
    reads as the safer option but forces every render site in the test suite —
    roughly twenty modules, plus the golden snapshots — to supply a variable that
    has nothing to do with what they assert. That is churn with its own breakage
    risk and no bearing on R6.6.

    `is defined and` is the SAME idiom `dqdl_rules` beside it already uses, for the
    same reason, and it fails CLOSED: an omitted variable disables the block, never
    enables it, which is the correct direction for a gate whose entire purpose is
    "do not turn this on for anyone who did not ask". Its jinja2-copies hazard is
    the one 2b.3 already analysed and dismissed — `is defined` only consults
    sentinel identity for an UNSET variable, and `code_assembly.generate_code`
    sets this one unconditionally on every production render, so it is answered by
    a real value. `test_dq_block_survives_a_second_jinja2_copy_in_sys_modules`
    covers it.

    THE COST, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. A render site that omits the variable
    gets DQ off even for glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2, whose production default is ON —
    so such a site no longer reflects production for that one template. The
    load-bearing guard is therefore upstream and mechanical:
    `test_every_p64_partial_variable_is_set_unconditionally_in_template_vars` reads
    the whole variable surface of every partial p64 owns — this one and
    `_dedup.py.j2` since 2c.3 — and fails if `data_quality_enabled` is ever missing
    from `template_vars`, assigned inside an `if`, or routed through the
    source_config/target_config splat. Loudness moved from the template to
    that test; it did not go away.

    THE FENCE THAT MOVING THIS BLOCK IS MOST LIKELY TO BREAK (p64 do-NOT 5 /
    R6.3). There are two sibling statement groups inside the `dqdl_rules` arm of
    the `evaluate` stage, and they are NOT nested:

      1. the EvaluateDataQuality call, wrapped in a `try:` whose handlers
         downgrade any failure to `logger.warning` — deliberately non-blocking,
         because a missing awsgluedq or a DQ service error must not fail a job
         whose data is fine;
      2. the row split and the quarantine parquet write, at the SAME indent as
         that `try:` and therefore OUTSIDE it.

    Group 2 must never migrate inside group 1's `try`. The natural cut points
    for an extraction are exactly the `try` edges, which is why this is called
    out here rather than left to the reader: once rules have evaluated WITH
    failures, a quarantine-write failure must fail the job rather than lose
    rows. Swallowed, it silently drops the failing rows — the row-loss defect
    the whole quarantine mechanism exists to prevent. The block carries its own
    "deliberately OUTSIDE the non-blocking try/except above" comment in the
    emitted Python; that comment is load-bearing, not decoration.

    RELATEDLY (R6.4): the `RuntimeError` that refuses to continue when rows
    failed and no quarantine bucket is configured is preserved VERBATIM —
    message text, wrapping and all. It is the fail-closed arm of the same
    guarantee. Do not reword it, re-indent it into a different string, or
    soften it to a warning.

    THE NON-PRINTABLE CHARACTER CHECK (p64 R8) FLAGS; IT DOES NOT QUARANTINE, AND
    ITS PREDICATE DOES NOT COME THROUGH THIS PARTIAL (p64 task 2d.1) — only its
    `checks[]` entry does, from the 'report' stage, as the later section on that
    entry's emission site explains. Recorded here
    rather than only at its own site, because this is the file a reader arrives
    at when they wonder why a second row-level check does not reuse the
    quarantine write below — and "reuse the existing mechanism" is the obvious
    first idea. R8.1 permits either arm ("flags or quarantines") and R8.3 is
    written conditionally ("IF the check quarantines"), so this was a live
    choice, not a reading of the requirement. Four facts decided it, and the
    first is about the code immediately below:

      1. NO DQDL RULE CAN FAIL ON A CONTROL CHARACTER, so the quarantine write
         is not on a live path for this signal. `_generate_dqdl_rules` emits
         exactly `RowCount > 0`, `Completeness "f" >= 0.95` and
         `Uniqueness "f" >= 0.95`; none inspects a character class (R8.2, which
         is why the check must be a Spark predicate at all). A row carrying NUL,
         BEL or ESC therefore passes every rule this system can express, so
         `_dq_fail_count` is 0 on precisely the runs the check exists to catch —
         and the quarantine write below sits inside
         `if _dq_fail_count > 0 and _dq_results is not None`. Quarantining would
         mean making that write reachable with `_dq_fail_count == 0`, i.e.
         restructuring the fenced region whose entire point (do-NOT 5 / R6.3) is
         that it is not entered casually. Flagging touches none of it.

      2. QUARANTINE COULD NOT HONOUR R8.1'S PER-PIPELINE OPT-IN ON FOUR OF THE
         FIVE TEMPLATES WITHOUT UNDOING 2b.4. The quarantine write renders only
         inside this file's `data_quality_enabled … and dqdl_rules` guard, and
         `code_assembly.resolve_data_quality_enabled` defaults that OFF for
         every template except `glue_job_s3_tables.py.j2`. So a JDBC-target
         pipeline that opts into the character check and leaves DQ alone would
         render a job containing no check — a silent no-op, which is the class
         of defect p64 exists to remove. Giving the check its own guard AND a
         path to this quarantine write would hand row-level splitting and a
         quarantine write to a target type that never had them: exactly the
         R6.6 reclassification `resolve_data_quality_enabled` was written to
         prevent. A flag needs neither — a predicate plus a count renders under
         its own `quality_config` guard on all five, independent of
         `data_quality_enabled`.

      3. QUARANTINE WOULD FOLD A THIRD ROW-LOSS CAUSE INTO `rows_quarantined`,
         which is the misattribution R7.6 forbade one task earlier. Read as "add
         to the same counter", R8.3 makes that number mean "rows that failed a
         DQDL rule UNION rows containing a control character". 2c.4 spent a whole
         task establishing the opposite standard — `rows_read - rows_written`
         stays decomposable, and dedup drops get their own `rows_deduplicated`
         key rather than being absorbed here. A flag keeps the decomposition
         intact by construction: no rows are lost, so there is no term to
         attribute. R8.3's "same report" is still honoured, as a `checks[]` entry
         on this same `(run_id, "data_quality")` row.

      4. THE REPORT ROW IS ONE `put_item`, SO A SECOND WRITER OVERWRITES RATHER
         THAN MERGES (`LGR-9e76249-3`: the Glue runtime role is PutItem-only on
         the state table, with no UpdateItem). A quarantining check wanting its
         own counters on this row would have to sit inside the `_dq_report` dict
         built in the 'report' stage — i.e. inside the DQ gate, back to (2) — or
         write a second row that clobbers the first. A `checks[]` entry is added
         to the dict already being built: one put_item, unchanged.

    WHAT THE REJECTED ARM WOULD HAVE DONE, AND TO WHOM. Quarantine diverts the
    row: it never reaches the target, and it lands under
    `s3://<bucket>/quarantine/<run_id>/`.
      * The operator who opted in loses rows. Control-character contamination is
        usually systematic — a bad export encoding, not a stray byte — so this is
        rarely a few rows; one BEL in a trailing notes column removes that whole
        row from the load, and a mis-encoded file removes all of them.
      * The report reader gets `overall_status: fail` on a run whose declared
        rules all passed, and a `rows_quarantined` that no longer answers "how
        many rows failed a data-quality rule".
      * `shared/load_outcome.py` gains a fourth unnamed term in
        `rows_read - rows_written`, so `derive_load_outcome` reports
        `partial_load` for a run whose only problem is encoding, with nothing on
        the row saying so.
      * Anyone with no `quarantine_bucket` configured gets a FAILING job rather
        than a flag, because quarantine inherits R6.4's `RuntimeError` above.
        Correct for DQ; a poor trade for opting into a character check.

    WHAT FLAGGING GIVES UP, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: the control characters
    reach the target. A NUL in a string column is a real defect for a downstream
    consumer and a flag does not stop it. Accepted, because the check is off by
    default so no existing pipeline's data changes; because the report finding
    this answers was that the contamination went UNDETECTED, and detection is
    what a flag delivers; and because the asymmetry of regret runs one way —
    quarantine stays available later against the same predicate and the same
    `quality_config` key, whereas walking back a quarantine that has been
    silently removing rows costs an operator data they cannot recover from a
    report. `LGR-9e76249-1` is the precedent in this same domain: `partial_load`
    was made display-only and a threshold that fails the run was rejected,
    because a signal must have a reader before it has a writer.

    CONSEQUENTLY, TWO THINGS THIS PARTIAL'S 'report' STAGE MUST NOT BE MADE TO
    DO. Its `overall_status` is derived from `_dq_fail_count` over DQDL rule
    outcomes; a flag that does not gate the write must not flip it, so the
    character check's `checks[]` entry carries status `warning` — the third
    value `QualityCheck.status` already admits, which
    `QualityReportCard.checkStatusType` already renders. And `_dq_rows_quarantined`
    stays the DQ split's own number: nothing from R8 is added to it.

    WHERE THAT ENTRY IS EMITTED, since 2d.2 built it: HERE, inline in the 'report'
    stage, between the DQDL rule loop and the `_dq_report` dict, behind its own
    `quality_config.nonprintable_check_enabled` guard. NOT from the check's own
    partial, and the reason is `LGR-9e76249-3` (the report row is one `put_item`
    on a PutItem-only role, so a second writer clobbers rather than merges): the
    entry has to be appended to the `_dq_checks` list already being built inside
    this file, which is not a point a caller can reach with an include. The check
    itself lives in `_partials/_nonprintable_check.py.j2`, included by each
    template after this partial's 'evaluate' site.

    THE TWO GUARDS MUST STAY IDENTICAL. The append below reads `_adi_np_rows`,
    which only the check partial defines, and both render on
    `quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("nonprintable_check_enabled")
    is true`. Change one expression without the other and this stage dereferences
    an undefined name — caught by the surrounding `except Exception as _ddb_err`,
    so no rows are lost, but the whole DQ report row silently stops being written.
    `test_nonprintable_check_all_targets.py` pins that both sites render together
    on all five templates.

    NOTE THE ASYMMETRY THAT FOLLOWS FROM THE CHECK HAVING ITS OWN GATE: the check
    renders wherever `nonprintable_check_enabled` is set, but this append renders
    only inside the `data_quality_enabled and dqdl_rules` arm below. A pipeline
    with the check on and DQ off therefore gets the count and the log line and no
    durable record — there is no report row for that pipeline at all. Stated in
    the check partial's header too; closing it needs a row of its own, which is a
    response-shape decision rather than a template one.

    REQUIRED CONTEXT VARIABLES: `dqdl_rules`, `quarantine_bucket`,
    `data_quality_enabled` and (since 2d.2) `quality_config`, all four assigned
    unconditionally in `code_assembly.generate_code`'s `template_vars`
    (R6.5 / do-NOT 6).

    REQUIRED CALLER VARIABLE: `data_quality_stage`, one of

      'evaluate' — after the transform and any auto-cast/column selection, and
                   BEFORE `transformed_count` is computed. Emits the evaluation,
                   the split and the quarantine write, and rebinds
                   `df_transformed` to the Passed rows.
      'report'   — AFTER `transformed_count` is computed. Emits the CloudWatch
                   counters and the `(run_id, "data_quality")` state row. Reads
                   `transformed_count`, `_dq_rows_quarantined`,
                   `_dq_quarantine_path`, `_dq_outcomes`, `_dq_fail_count`,
                   `_dq_total_count`, `_dq_run_id`, `_dq_pipeline_id` and
                   `_dq_state_table`, all of which the 'evaluate' stage defines.

    THE TWO STAGES ARE ORDERED AND BOTH ARE REQUIRED. 'report' reads locals
    that only 'evaluate' assigns, and it must sit after `transformed_count`
    because `rows_passed` IS `transformed_count`. The three lines between them —
    the blank line, `transformed_count = df_transformed.count()` and its logger
    — are NOT part of this partial: they render whether or not any DQL rule
    exists, so they belong to the caller.

    NO DEFAULT STAGE, and the `else` arm below dereferences a name that is
    deliberately never defined. With StrictUndefined an omitted OR MISTYPED
    stage then fails the render loudly, naming itself in the error. Following
    `_partials/_load_counts.py.j2`, which takes `load_counts_stage` the same
    way and for the same reason. A silent fall-through would be worse here than
    a wrong value: a mistyped stage would render NOTHING, so the DQ block would
    simply vanish from a template that appears to include it — which is the
    exact class of gap R6.1 exists to close.

    INDENTATION IS LITERAL, NOT APPLIED BY A FILTER. The body is written at the
    4-space `try:`-body indent it needs, rather than at column 0 inside a
    `filter indent(4)` the way `_load_counts.py.j2` does it. That is a
    correctness choice, not a style one: `dqdl_rules` is interpolated into a
    triple-quoted `dq_ruleset` literal, and a real ruleset is multi-line, so an
    `indent` filter would add leading spaces to its continuation lines and
    change the DQDL text actually evaluated at runtime. The `_write_*.py.j2`
    partials carry literal indentation for comparable reasons, so both idioms
    already exist in this directory.

    ALL of the rationale lives in this Jinja comment and none of it in the
    emitted Python, per `LGR-b46a5bb-4` (template / ratified): this repo's
    template fences are substring assertions over the RENDERED output, so
    naming a fenced token in emitted Python would be counted as an occurrence
    of it.
-#}
{%- if data_quality_stage not in ('evaluate', 'report') -%}
{{ data_quality_stage_must_be_either_evaluate_or_report }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if data_quality_stage == 'evaluate' -%}
{# indent-preserving spacer, deliberate — see header #}    # === Glue Data Quality Evaluation ===
{% if data_quality_enabled is defined and data_quality_enabled and dqdl_rules is defined and dqdl_rules %}
    # Runtime metadata (--run_id / --pipeline_id / --state_table) is resolved
    # through the single _runtime_arg mechanism (getResolvedOptions gated on
    # sys.argv presence, P28 R19.9 — see _runtime_args above). Manual runs fall
    # back to the Glue-provided JOB_RUN_ID so quarantined rows remain locatable
    # per run.
    _dq_run_id = _adi_run_id
    _dq_pipeline_id = _runtime_arg("pipeline_id")
    _dq_state_table = _runtime_arg("state_table", "agentic_data_ingestion_state")
    _dq_quarantine_run_id = _adi_effective_run_id
    _dq_quarantine_bucket = "{{ quarantine_bucket }}"

    _dq_fail_count = 0
    _dq_total_count = 0
    _dq_outcomes = []
    _dq_results = None
    try:
        from awsglue.dynamicframe import DynamicFrame
        from awsgluedq.transforms import EvaluateDataQuality

        dq_ruleset = """{{ dqdl_rules }}"""

        dq_dyf = DynamicFrame.fromDF(df_transformed, glue_context, "dq_input")
        dq_results = EvaluateDataQuality().process_rows(
            frame=dq_dyf,
            ruleset=dq_ruleset,
            publishing_options={
                "dataQualityEvaluationContext": args['JOB_NAME'],
                "enableDataQualityCloudWatchMetrics": True,
                "enableDataQualityResultsPublishing": True,
            },
            additional_options={
                "performanceTuning.caching": "CACHE_NOTHING",
                "publishAggregatedMetrics.status": "ENABLED",
            },
        )

        # Collect rule outcomes (small dataset — one row per rule, safe to collect)
        from awsglue.transforms import SelectFromCollection
        rule_outcomes = SelectFromCollection.apply(dfc=dq_results, key="ruleOutcomes")
        _dq_outcomes = rule_outcomes.toDF().collect()

        logger.info("=== Data Quality Results ===")
        for row in _dq_outcomes:
            outcome = row["Outcome"]
            rule = row["Rule"]
            reason = row["FailureReason"] if row["FailureReason"] else ""
            logger.info(f"  [{outcome}] {rule} {reason}")

        _dq_fail_count = sum(1 for r in _dq_outcomes if r["Outcome"] == "Failed")
        _dq_total_count = len(_dq_outcomes)
        logger.info(f"Data Quality: {_dq_total_count - _dq_fail_count}/{_dq_total_count} rules passed")
        _dq_results = dq_results

    except ImportError:
        logger.warning("awsgluedq not available — skipping inline data quality evaluation")
    except Exception as _dq_err:
        logger.warning(f"Data quality evaluation failed (non-blocking): {_dq_err}")

    # Split row-level outcomes (P28 R11.1): Passed rows continue to the target
    # write; Failed rows are QUARANTINED to quarantine/<run_id>/ — never
    # silently dropped. This block is deliberately OUTSIDE the non-blocking
    # try/except above: once rules have evaluated with failures, a
    # quarantine-write failure must fail the job rather than lose rows.
    _dq_rows_quarantined = 0
    _dq_quarantine_path = ""
    if _dq_fail_count > 0 and _dq_results is not None:
        from awsglue.transforms import SelectFromCollection
        _dq_row_outcomes = SelectFromCollection.apply(dfc=_dq_results, key="rowLevelOutcomes").toDF()
        _dq_failed_df = _dq_row_outcomes.filter("DataQualityEvaluationResult != 'Passed'")
        _dq_passed_df = _dq_row_outcomes.filter("DataQualityEvaluationResult = 'Passed'")
        _dq_meta_cols = ["DataQualityRulesPass", "DataQualityRulesFail", "DataQualityRulesSkip", "DataQualityEvaluationResult"]
        for _dq_col in _dq_meta_cols:
            if _dq_col in _dq_failed_df.columns:
                _dq_failed_df = _dq_failed_df.drop(_dq_col)
            if _dq_col in _dq_passed_df.columns:
                _dq_passed_df = _dq_passed_df.drop(_dq_col)
        # Rule-level failures do not always map to row-level failures (e.g. a
        # dataset-level RowCount rule) — only write quarantine parquet when
        # failing rows actually exist (no empty objects).
        _dq_rows_quarantined = _dq_failed_df.count()
        if _dq_rows_quarantined > 0:
            if not _dq_quarantine_bucket:
                raise RuntimeError(
                    "Data quality rows failed but no quarantine bucket is configured — "
                    "refusing to silently drop failing rows"
                )
            _dq_quarantine_path = f"s3://{_dq_quarantine_bucket}/quarantine/{_dq_quarantine_run_id}/"
            _dq_failed_df.write.mode("overwrite").parquet(_dq_quarantine_path)
            logger.warning(
                f"Data Quality: {_dq_fail_count} rule(s) FAILED — quarantined {_dq_rows_quarantined} failing row(s) to {_dq_quarantine_path}"
            )
        else:
            logger.info("Data Quality: no row-level failures — nothing to quarantine")
        df_transformed = _dq_passed_df
{% endif %}
    # === End Data Quality ===
{%- endif -%}
{%- if data_quality_stage == 'report' -%}
{% if data_quality_enabled is defined and data_quality_enabled and dqdl_rules is defined and dqdl_rules %}
    # === Data Quality counters → job metrics + run state record (P28 R11.1) ===
    # rows_passed == transformed_count: df_transformed is the Passed split when
    # any rule failed, or the full frame when all rules passed. Counters are
    # recorded even when rows_quarantined == 0. Emission is best-effort
    # telemetry — the row-loss guarantee is the quarantine write above.
    if _dq_outcomes:
        try:
            import boto3 as _boto3
            _boto3.client("cloudwatch").put_metric_data(
                Namespace="AgenticDataIngestion/DataQuality",
                MetricData=[
                    {
                        "MetricName": "RowsPassed",
                        "Value": float(transformed_count),
                        "Unit": "Count",
                        "Dimensions": [{"Name": "JobName", "Value": args['JOB_NAME']}],
                    },
                    {
                        "MetricName": "RowsQuarantined",
                        "Value": float(_dq_rows_quarantined),
                        "Unit": "Count",
                        "Dimensions": [{"Name": "JobName", "Value": args['JOB_NAME']}],
                    },
                ],
            )
            logger.info(f"DQ counters published: rows_passed={transformed_count}, rows_quarantined={_dq_rows_quarantined}")
        except Exception as _cw_err:
            logger.warning(f"Failed to publish DQ counters to CloudWatch: {_cw_err}")

    # Write DQ results (including quarantine counters) to DynamoDB for dashboard display
    if _dq_outcomes and _dq_run_id:
        try:
            import boto3 as _boto3
            import json as _json
            from datetime import datetime as _dt, timezone as _tz
            from decimal import Decimal as _Decimal

            _has_metrics = len(_dq_outcomes) > 0 and "EvaluatedMetrics" in _dq_outcomes[0].asDict()
            _dq_checks = []
            for _row in _dq_outcomes:
                _check = {"name": _row["Rule"], "status": "pass" if _row["Outcome"] == "Passed" else "fail"}
                if _row["FailureReason"]:
                    _check["failure_reason"] = _row["FailureReason"]
                if _has_metrics:
                    _metrics = _row["EvaluatedMetrics"]
                    if _metrics:
                        for _mk, _mv in _metrics.items():
                            _check["actual"] = float(_mv) if _mv is not None else None
                _dq_checks.append(_check)

{% if quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("nonprintable_check_enabled") is true %}            # p64 R8.1/R8.3 — the non-printable character check's finding, on
            # THIS report rather than a second row. Status is `warning`, never
            # `fail`: the check flags and diverts nothing, so it must not move
            # overall_status (derived from the DQDL rule outcomes above) and it
            # adds nothing to rows_quarantined. It is also not counted in
            # rules_passed/rules_failed/rules_total, which describe the DQDL
            # ruleset only. `_adi_np_rows` is defined by the check block, which
            # renders under this same condition.
            _dq_np_check = {
                "name": "NonPrintableCharacters",
                "status": "warning" if _adi_np_rows > 0 else "pass",
                "actual": float(_adi_np_rows),
            }
            if _adi_np_rows > 0:
                _dq_np_check["failure_reason"] = (
                    f"{_adi_np_rows} row(s) written to the target contain control characters "
                    "(flagged, not quarantined)"
                )
            _dq_checks.append(_dq_np_check)

{% endif %}{% if quality_config is mapping and quality_config.get("cast_null_check_enabled") is true %}            # p64 R9.1/R9.3 — the type-cast failure count, on THIS report
            # rather than a second row, and directly beside the Completeness
            # outcome it corrects. Status is `warning`, never `fail`: no row was
            # removed from the frame, so this must not move overall_status and
            # adds nothing to rows_quarantined. It is counted in none of
            # rules_passed/rules_failed/rules_total, which describe the DQDL
            # ruleset only.
            #
            # The locals() test is REQUIRED, not defensive clutter. The count is
            # only computable where an auto-cast block exists, so this partial
            # renders on templates where the check block does not. Without the
            # test those templates raise NameError inside the enclosing
            # try/except and lose the ENTIRE report row silently. An unmeasured
            # run gets NO entry — absent and zero are different claims, and "no
            # conversion nulled a value" is not something an unmeasured run can
            # say.
            if "_adi_cast_null_cells" in locals():
                _dq_cast_check = {
                    "name": "TypeCastFailures",
                    "status": "warning" if _adi_cast_null_cells > 0 else "pass",
                    "actual": float(_adi_cast_null_cells),
                }
                if _adi_cast_null_cells > 0:
                    _dq_cast_check["failure_reason"] = (
                        f"{_adi_cast_null_cells} value(s) became NULL converting to the target "
                        f"type ({_adi_cast_null_detail}) — these had a source value and are not "
                        f"empty sources, which the completeness rule cannot distinguish. "
                        f"Rows were kept."
                    )
                _dq_checks.append(_dq_cast_check)

{% endif %}            _dq_report = {
                "run_id": _dq_run_id, "step_name": "data_quality",
                "pipeline_id": _dq_pipeline_id,
                "overall_status": "pass" if _dq_fail_count == 0 else "fail",
                "checks": _dq_checks, "root_cause": None if _dq_fail_count == 0 else "data_issue",
                "recommendations": [] if _dq_fail_count == 0 else ["Review failed data quality rules"],
                "source": "glue_inline_dq", "timestamp": _dt.now(_tz.utc).isoformat(),
                "rules_passed": _dq_total_count - _dq_fail_count,
                "rules_failed": _dq_fail_count, "rules_total": _dq_total_count,
                "rows_passed": transformed_count,
                "rows_quarantined": _dq_rows_quarantined,
                "quarantine_path": _dq_quarantine_path or None,
            }
            _tbl = _boto3.resource("dynamodb").Table(_dq_state_table)
            _tbl.put_item(Item=_json.loads(_json.dumps(_dq_report, default=str), parse_float=_Decimal))
            logger.info(f"DQ report written to DynamoDB for run {_dq_run_id}")
        except Exception as _ddb_err:
            logger.warning(f"Failed to write DQ results to DynamoDB: {_ddb_err}")
    # === End Data Quality counters ===
